        response: `In megacities, the lack of housing is becoming a major issue leading to overcrowding, homelessness and rising living costs. Some people believe that only governments are able to solving this problem. I strongly agree that governments should take the leading, although businessmen and individuals can also contribute.

In the world, many people face the problem of limited number of houses they can dwell in. This condition leads to high rent-prices people have to pay. Take New York, for example, many residents spend almost half of their monthly income on rent due to the severe shortage of affordable housing. As a result, they may be unable to afford other major expenses, such as travelling or buying a new car. Furthermore, people may have to spend hours commuting work because there is no affordable housing available near their workplaces. This can be exhausting and deprive them of valuable time that could otherwise be spent with their families. To address this problem, local authorities should launch programmes aimed at supporting and developing countrysides. In this way, they can encourage people to move to these areas by providing them with new employment opportunities. Therefore, the local economy can be strengthened, thereby improving the standard of living of the local population.

However, investing in rural development can be a profitable business opportunity as well as an effective solution to the housing problem, since it can create prosperous communities outside urban areas. A variety of new enterprises can be established in deprived rural areas, encouraging people to move away from cities, which can reduce the need for living in these regions. For instance, one plant opened in village can provide employees with thousands of new positions. If this strategy is implemented effectively, cities may become less densely populated. Therefore, this approach should not be overlooked.

In conclusion, although I accept that governments play a crucial role in addressing the housing shortage, I believe that businesses and private investors can also make a significant contribution to solving this problem.`
